<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kikaijima</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kikai Island</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kikaijima is a small island of the Amami group, east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amami-oshima">Amami Ōshima</a>, now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagoshima-prefecture">Kagoshima Prefecture</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. It resisted the expansion of the Ryukyu Kingdom until King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sho-toku">Shō Toku</a> sent an expedition in 1466 that brought it under Shuri's control. Archaeological finds show that it was an early link between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a> and the southern islands.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kikaijima.jpg" alt="Kikaijima" width="320" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Image Science and Analysis Laboratory, NASA-Johnson Space Center. &quot;The Gateway to Astronaut Photography of Earth.&quot; · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kikaijima.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
